  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1218 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nThe extended pattern shows little relief from the heat as the high \npressure dome wobbles anywhere from the southern High Plains into \nthe Four Corners. If there is any consolation, the H5 high is not \nprogged to reach 600 meters until late next week, so this should \nkeep our heat wave in the low-to-mid 100s with excessive heat (110 \nor hotter) restricted to isolated occurrences. Still, this pattern \nof high heat, low relative humidity, and warmer overnight lows fits \nthe bill for a flash drought event that may severely stress area \ncrops. The only prospect for rainfall exists Friday evening and \novernight when the center of the high will be farthest west from the \narea allowing a shortwave trough in northwest flow to impact the TX \nPanhandle, perhaps even farther south. What storms do form won't \nhave much steering flow available, so locally heavy rain would be in \nthe offing. Global guidance and ensembles suggest this lingering mid-\nlevel moisture with any remnant MCVs could yield another round of \nstorms Saturday afternoon or evening, but the NBM is mostly dry \naside from a sliver of PoPs in the far southwest TX Panhandle. \nThereafter, the high rebuilds and elongates from the Four Corners to \nthe Red River while holding between 595 and 598 dam. This keeps our \nhighs in the low/mid 100s through early next week.",
